      "credit": "This image has been extracted from another file",
      "source": "Messengers of Genghis Khan at the gates of Nishapur. Supplément Persan 1443 f.94",
      "description": "Messengers of Genghis Khan at the gates of Nishapur. Supplément Persan 1443 f.94\n\"94. Genghis Khan on horseback, followed by three Mongol officers in front of the walls of the city of Nishapur; two Muslims stand on the walls; Djingiz is armed with a bow and a quiver; one of the Mongol horsemen holds a standard on which we see the current coat of arms of the Qajars, the sun rising behind a lion.\" Revue des bibliothèques, Volume 10, p.190\n\"The earliest-known representation [of the sun and lion] as a banner device (Figure 18) is from a miniature painting illustrating a copy, dated 826/1423, of the Šāh-nāma of Šams-al-Dīn Kāšānī—an epic composition on the Mongol conquest (Bibliothèque Nationale, suppl. no. 1443). It shows several (Mongol?) horsemen approaching a walled city (Nīšāpūr), and one of them carries a tall banner which is crowned by a spear-head within a crescent moon (the māhča-ye lewāʾ) and its square background bears a lion passant with a rising sun on its back and two floating pennons (Blochet, 1928, III, p. 180). A similar early depiction (Figure 19) is on a large, double-paged miniature dated ca. 865/1460 (Martin, Pls. 60 f.).\" Encyclopedia Iranica",
